00:18:12.172 --> 00:18:14.592
People come to see opera and ballet.

00:18:15.192 --> 00:18:18.933
It was very exceptional to have an evening entirely devoted to ballet.

00:18:19.413 --> 00:18:23.695
He will impose a weekly ballet evening starting in 1935.

00:18:24.115 --> 00:18:24.615
After that,

00:18:24.815 --> 00:18:28.196
He demanded two evenings during the week.

00:18:28.197 --> 00:18:31.237
He did it on Wednesday and Friday.

00:18:31.917 --> 00:18:32.337
After that,

00:18:32.337 --> 00:18:33.978
We had the entire month of July,

00:18:33.979 --> 00:18:35.278
which was ballet month.

00:18:35.898 --> 00:18:38.860
But he had an ally,

00:18:39.281 --> 00:18:40.822
a very favorable ally,

00:18:40.823 --> 00:18:41.242
IFAR,

00:18:41.242 --> 00:18:42.243
It's an economic crisis.

00:18:42.764 --> 00:18:44.665
And dance is always cheaper than opera.

00:18:52.111 --> 00:18:54.133
The situation is difficult during this period.

00:18:55.394 --> 00:18:59.137
More and more French people are losing their jobs following the 1929 crisis.

00:19:01.039 --> 00:19:04.962
Criticism is being raised, pointing to the excessive number of Russians within the Paris Opera.

00:19:06.010 --> 00:19:06.831
Tensions are rising.

00:19:07.931 --> 00:19:09.953
We still need to put ourselves back in the situation of the time.

00:19:10.013 --> 00:19:10.513
First of all,

00:19:10.853 --> 00:19:11.994
Liffard was not French.

00:19:12.234 --> 00:19:13.195
He was stateless.

00:19:14.256 --> 00:19:15.376
They made him feel it,

00:19:15.577 --> 00:19:16.077
in Paris,

00:19:16.217 --> 00:19:17.118
that he was a foreigner.

00:19:17.338 --> 00:19:18.859
Let's not forget that in the 1930s,

00:19:18.939 --> 00:19:22.221
There is the rise in power of the leagues,

00:19:22.802 --> 00:19:23.862
the far right,

00:19:23.982 --> 00:19:24.743
xenophobic,

00:19:24.763 --> 00:19:25.263
etc.

00:19:25.623 --> 00:19:27.585
And Liffard was the foreigner.

00:19:29.206 --> 00:19:30.287
In this climate of hostility,

00:19:31.127 --> 00:19:35.070
Liffard, however, manages to stage a show that will save his place at the opera.

00:19:36.450 --> 00:19:37.591
This is Icarus,

00:19:38.532 --> 00:19:43.497
a project that IFAR has been developing for a few years and for which Paul Lartre will design the sets.

00:19:55.909 --> 00:19:56.810
Inspired by the myth,

00:19:57.591 --> 00:20:00.654
It tells the story of a winged man who flies towards the sun.

00:20:09.335 --> 00:20:09.835
THANKS.

00:20:11.787 --> 00:20:13.368
The ballet reflects its era.

00:20:14.208 --> 00:20:17.890
Civil aviation experienced a resounding boom during this period.

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:21.192
The press reports on the exploits of pilots like Charles Lindbergh,

00:20:21.592 --> 00:20:22.952
who has just crossed the Atlantic,

00:20:23.273 --> 00:20:23.993
and Saint-Exupéry,

00:20:24.053 --> 00:20:26.094
who describes his flights to Africa in his novels.

00:20:26.414 --> 00:20:26.814
Actually,

00:20:26.814 --> 00:20:32.937
This ballet, created in 1936, is a monument of modernity.

00:20:33.898 --> 00:20:36.539
of Master Icard, almost naked.

00:20:38.427 --> 00:20:39.568
With accessories,

00:20:39.608 --> 00:20:40.309
wings,

00:20:40.329 --> 00:20:40.990
things like that,

00:20:41.270 --> 00:20:43.612
It was truly revolutionary at the time.

00:20:56.505 --> 00:20:58.687
Serge Liffard did this

00:20:58.707 --> 00:21:02.170
He choreographed and composed the music himself.

00:21:03.311 --> 00:21:06.113
He had a key and he tapped out all the rhythms.

00:21:06.934 --> 00:21:15.660
And when he gave these rhythms to Honegger to set to music,

00:21:16.821 --> 00:21:18.402
It was done using only percussion instruments.

00:21:19.743 --> 00:21:20.803
That,
that was a revolution.

00:21:20.904 --> 00:21:21.404
For him,

00:21:21.444 --> 00:21:25.106
It is the choreographer who must first conceive their choreography in silence.

00:21:25.167 --> 00:21:26.527
Then come the other arts.

00:21:26.848 --> 00:21:27.948
There is a power struggle at play.

00:21:27.968 --> 00:21:31.411
There is a question of precedence, in a way.

00:21:31.699 --> 00:21:33.460
He wants to assert the primacy of dance,

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:35.801
always the primary element in his mind.

00:21:36.501 --> 00:21:40.823
He once told me that man's first art,

00:21:41.943 --> 00:21:42.664
It's the dance.

00:21:44.945 --> 00:21:45.465
I was telling him,

00:21:45.625 --> 00:21:47.145
Don't you think it's the music?

00:21:47.205 --> 00:21:47.705
master ?

00:21:47.906 --> 00:21:48.306
He told me,

00:21:48.306 --> 00:21:48.806
No,

00:21:49.246 --> 00:21:50.307
It's the dance.

00:21:50.527 --> 00:21:51.087
the movement.

00:22:00.971 --> 00:22:07.194
He convinces all his detractors in Icard by embodying a hero who dies and loses.

00:22:08.175 --> 00:22:09.255
So I think it's quite pretty.

00:22:10.916 --> 00:22:12.177
Liffard is a superstar.

00:22:12.997 --> 00:22:15.798
And this idol status is characteristic of his modernity.

00:22:16.819 --> 00:22:17.579
Egocentrism,

00:22:18.019 --> 00:22:18.740
social evening,

00:22:19.340 --> 00:22:20.681
overexposure of her body,

00:22:21.241 --> 00:22:22.862
Serge Liffard was a celebrity before his time.

00:22:23.342 --> 00:22:25.043
And its power of attraction is immediate.

00:22:29.843 --> 00:22:30.964
He announces his plans.

00:22:31.224 --> 00:22:32.285
Can we imagine today,

00:22:32.986 --> 00:22:33.606
Release,

00:22:34.127 --> 00:22:35.288
open three columns,

00:22:35.468 --> 00:22:39.531
two,
three columns to a choreographer who is going to announce his future creation.

00:22:40.151 --> 00:22:41.633
So he has a very high level of fame.

00:22:42.153 --> 00:22:43.654
He loved being photographed,

00:22:43.794 --> 00:22:48.498
He loved being with not only the political world but also the world of artists.

00:22:48.919 --> 00:22:51.601
So obviously he was so adored,

00:22:51.761 --> 00:22:54.623
So naturally, that inflates the character a bit.

00:22:55.224 --> 00:22:56.445
When he mounted Prometheus,

00:22:56.985 --> 00:22:59.047
He had a heated argument with the conductor.

00:22:59.267 --> 00:23:00.927
because he wanted to add a chord.

00:23:01.588 --> 00:23:02.188
So we told him no.

00:23:02.189 --> 00:23:03.868
Beethoven's score must not be touched.

00:23:04.308 --> 00:23:04.749
But he said,

00:23:04.749 --> 00:23:05.669
Beethoven was famous,

00:23:05.869 --> 00:23:06.729
But Serge Liffard too.

00:23:08.290 --> 00:23:09.130
Apart from Icard,

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:12.951
Serge Liffard's innovations have received mixed reviews from critics.

00:23:14.251 --> 00:23:15.452
While they are sometimes harsh,

00:23:16.072 --> 00:23:18.112
However, the criticisms only have an artistic scope.

00:23:19.413 --> 00:23:21.113
World War II will change everything.

00:23:22.153 --> 00:23:23.654
and make it significantly more dangerous.

00:23:40.583 --> 00:23:42.604
A period of uncertainty is being observed at the Paris Opera.

00:23:44.184 --> 00:23:47.066
Following the general mobilization of September 3, 1939,

00:23:48.026 --> 00:23:49.687
Many dancers are being requisitioned.

00:23:51.207 --> 00:23:51.748
Luckily,

00:23:52.468 --> 00:23:55.569
This mobilization does not concern artists of foreign nationality.

00:23:57.150 --> 00:23:58.190
The Nansen passport,

00:23:58.430 --> 00:24:00.511
delivered to Serge Liffard a few years earlier,

00:24:01.131 --> 00:24:03.472
thus exempting the choreographer from any military service.

00:24:06.533 --> 00:24:10.175
Serge Liffard continues his life as a dancer and choreographer and performs in Australia.

00:24:10.667 --> 00:24:13.168
where he undertook a tour from January to March 1940.

00:24:14.229 --> 00:24:15.709
It features the Spectre de la Rose,

00:24:16.170 --> 00:24:17.971
The Lake of Signs and Icarus.

00:24:29.096 --> 00:24:31.017
We had never seen a dancer like Liffard before,

00:24:31.877 --> 00:24:32.638
also athletic,

00:24:33.198 --> 00:24:34.839
wild and expressive.

00:24:39.539 --> 00:24:42.542
There have been more than 25 encores during the biggest performances.

00:24:43.984 --> 00:24:46.387
Serge Liffard is making headlines in the Australian press.

00:24:47.428 --> 00:24:48.469
From Melbourne to Sydney,

00:24:48.809 --> 00:24:49.670
He's all anyone is talking about.

00:24:54.124 --> 00:24:56.085
1940,

00:24:56.086 --> 00:24:58.105
Nazi Germany expands its empire in Europe.

00:24:59.706 --> 00:25:01.626
Northern France and then Paris are occupied.

00:25:04.627 --> 00:25:05.767
In the heart of the capital,

00:25:06.467 --> 00:25:11.689
The pas de deux introduced by Liffard in his brooms are replaced by the rhythmic step of German boots.

00:25:14.630 --> 00:25:18.611
Shortly after his return to France and the signing of the armistice on June 22, 1940,

00:25:19.311 --> 00:25:22.672
The city of Paris will entrust Serge Liffard with a role of great importance.

00:25:23.692 --> 00:25:42.837
To reopen the Garnier opera house to prevent the Germans from occupying the building.

00:25:53.288 --> 00:26:00.632
Destroy the opera house and bring IFAR back to Berlin and build a dance school and ballet company in Berlin.

00:26:04.454 --> 00:26:05.015
For IFAR,

00:26:05.735 --> 00:26:06.976
A troubled period begins.

00:26:08.797 --> 00:26:11.178
Forced to deal with the Germans who occupied the capital,

00:26:11.758 --> 00:26:14.700
The choreographer first received Goebbels at the Paris Opera.

00:26:25.365 --> 00:26:26.066
Then it was Hitler.

00:26:26.326 --> 00:26:26.927
in person,

00:26:27.227 --> 00:26:29.909
who then visits the opera house during a trip to the capital.

00:26:31.771 --> 00:26:34.513
It is 6:30 in the morning when the Führer sets foot in the building.

00:26:35.494 --> 00:26:37.095
Having been warned of the dictator's visit,

00:26:37.636 --> 00:26:39.838
Liffard did not show up for work that morning.

00:26:42.200 --> 00:26:44.081
But the public is unaware of this absence.

00:26:44.882 --> 00:26:46.784
and thinks that the choreographer is fraternizing with the enemy.

00:26:47.644 --> 00:26:49.386
Traumatized by the arrival of German troops,

00:26:49.806 --> 00:26:52.048
The country's distrust of Serge Liffard is taking hold.

00:26:53.384 --> 00:26:53.985
It's later,

00:26:54.385 --> 00:26:55.426
in 1941,

00:26:55.846 --> 00:26:58.328
that Serge Liffard truly meets the dictator.

00:26:59.830 --> 00:27:02.252
The interview takes place in an office at the Führer's headquarters.

00:27:04.934 --> 00:27:10.438
He had been summoned to Berlin by Hitler, who asked him to create a grand ballet.

00:27:11.199 --> 00:27:15.643
a grand choreographic spectacle glorifying the Third Reich,

00:27:16.403 --> 00:27:17.164
which he refused.

00:27:18.065 --> 00:27:20.267
Accusations are starting to rain down in the capital.

00:27:21.368 --> 00:27:21.868
Yet...

00:27:22.444 --> 00:27:24.865
Serge Liffard's understanding with the enemy is not so obvious.

00:27:26.286 --> 00:27:27.647
Obsessed with racist ideals,

00:27:28.167 --> 00:27:30.669
The Germans have doubts about Serge Liffard's origins.

00:27:31.949 --> 00:27:33.290
He was then quickly asked,

00:27:33.390 --> 00:27:34.591
as with many other artists,

00:27:35.911 --> 00:27:37.212
a certificate of Arianism.

00:27:39.033 --> 00:27:42.995
Liffard was accused of being Jewish for a long time.

00:27:43.556 --> 00:27:43.996
And then,

00:27:43.996 --> 00:27:47.117
There was a faction of the SS who wanted him dead.

00:27:47.718 --> 00:27:51.060
Because people said Liffard's face was Jewish,

00:27:51.160 --> 00:27:51.660
etc.

00:27:52.232 --> 00:27:53.353
He finally showed,

00:27:53.413 --> 00:27:56.114
particularly through his brother's final year of high school

00:27:56.775 --> 00:28:01.477
through physical and blood analyses,

00:28:01.497 --> 00:28:01.997
etc.

00:28:02.278 --> 00:28:06.000
He was able to get rid of the accusation of being Jewish.

00:28:06.860 --> 00:28:07.821
Cleared of all suspicion,

00:28:08.501 --> 00:28:10.522
Serge Liffard continues his mission at the Paris Opera.

00:28:11.543 --> 00:28:12.403
Enjoying taking care of everything,

00:28:12.843 --> 00:28:15.085
The young prodigy wears many hats.

00:28:17.106 --> 00:28:18.687
He holds the positions of administrator,

00:28:19.307 --> 00:28:20.347
but also as a concierge.

00:28:20.688 --> 00:28:21.188
sweeper.

00:28:21.644 --> 00:28:22.285
telephonist,

00:28:22.726 --> 00:28:23.347
electrician,

00:28:23.928 --> 00:28:24.449
dancer,

00:28:25.010 --> 00:28:27.794
choreographer and of course ballet master.

00:28:38.855 --> 00:28:40.357
He wanted a very quick death.

00:28:42.013 --> 00:28:43.654
He died very quickly.

00:28:43.655 --> 00:28:45.214
In one second,

00:28:45.334 --> 00:28:46.435
It was stolen.

00:28:47.595 --> 00:28:49.435
With the help of his great friend Jean Cocteau,

00:28:50.056 --> 00:28:53.037
Liffard reintroduced danced poems to the opera stage.

00:28:54.357 --> 00:28:56.518
Even though Cocteau found the choreographer somewhat superficial,

00:28:57.138 --> 00:29:00.239
A certain originality of character brings these two great artists together.

00:29:01.379 --> 00:29:02.079
During the war,

00:29:02.539 --> 00:29:04.380
if they have to work together after curfew,

00:29:04.800 --> 00:29:07.881
Liffard whistles the tune of dear chance under Cocteau's window.

00:29:10.153 --> 00:29:11.193
In the space of 4 years,

00:29:11.693 --> 00:29:12.914
Liffard composed 12 ballets,

00:29:13.134 --> 00:29:13.954
including 6 original creations.

00:29:14.994 --> 00:29:16.115
During these performances,

00:29:16.715 --> 00:29:20.896
The rooms are half filled by soldiers or high-ranking officials of the German army.

00:29:23.537 --> 00:29:24.717
When I returned to the opera,

00:29:25.177 --> 00:29:27.498
The Germans were all in the orchestra.

00:29:27.538 --> 00:29:28.618
It was a green carpet.

00:29:29.299 --> 00:29:32.719
So we were obliged to dance there for the shows,

00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:36.100
because they had actually asked for the theatre to be reopened for them.

00:29:37.081 --> 00:29:37.921
After the shows,

00:29:38.633 --> 00:29:43.476
Serge Liffard meets the cream of the German army in the capital's grand restaurants such as Maxim's or La Pérouse.

00:29:44.696 --> 00:29:45.297
On the surface,

00:29:45.757 --> 00:29:48.378
Liffard exhibits all the characteristics of a supporter of the Reich.

00:29:49.539 --> 00:29:51.880
a man who regularly shares good meals with Nazis,

00:29:52.361 --> 00:29:54.222
whom he also entertains during his shows.

00:29:57.944 --> 00:29:59.505
He didn't necessarily have only friends,

00:29:59.545 --> 00:30:02.726
So we immediately categorized him as a collaborator.

00:30:02.826 --> 00:30:03.326
etc.

00:30:03.347 --> 00:30:04.927
I think we've all been there,

00:30:05.068 --> 00:30:05.568
Me,

00:30:05.868 --> 00:30:07.329
at my own small level.

00:30:07.729 --> 00:30:11.090
I was also a collaborator because I did all the shows.

00:30:16.012 --> 00:30:17.912
Besides his high-society role that causes a stir,

00:30:18.992 --> 00:30:21.613
Liffard presents another side of himself during the occupation.

00:30:22.714 --> 00:30:24.734
more secret and more glorious.

00:30:25.715 --> 00:30:28.996
It is that of a generous and empathetic man who participates in the war effort.

00:30:30.376 --> 00:30:33.537
He began by offering his help to the dancers of the Paris Opera.

00:30:37.453 --> 00:30:39.154
He had done a lot for the dancers,

00:30:40.154 --> 00:30:41.075
even during the war,

00:30:41.076 --> 00:30:44.797
where there were problems with dancers who were Israelites and whom he hid.

00:30:45.277 --> 00:30:51.301
He has a male dancer's condition that prevented him from going on compulsory military service.

00:30:51.461 --> 00:30:53.342
because he said at the time,

00:30:53.422 --> 00:30:54.122
If you take them,

00:30:54.123 --> 00:30:55.143
There are no more ballet performances.

00:30:55.243 --> 00:30:55.943
It's very simple.

00:30:57.484 --> 00:31:00.606
Serge Liffard continues to send large sums of money to various organizations.

00:31:01.226 --> 00:31:03.447
in support of children who are victims of war or prisoners.

00:31:04.548 --> 00:31:06.969
These sums are raised during countless galas.

00:31:07.109 --> 00:31:08.750
in which Liffard participated during the war.

00:31:10.311 --> 00:31:15.555
The dancer-choreographer puts his love of dance at the service of the French and thus responds to their needs for assistance.

00:31:17.657 --> 00:31:24.422
He was eager to gain recognition for ballet.

00:31:25.003 --> 00:31:30.407
His main goal was to make it known to the general public and explain that it is an art form.

00:31:30.447 --> 00:31:32.889
that we need the equivalent of...

00:31:33.901 --> 00:31:34.901
music,

00:31:35.041 --> 00:31:35.622
comedy,

00:31:35.642 --> 00:31:36.202
of writing,

00:31:36.203 --> 00:31:36.922
literature.

00:31:37.342 --> 00:31:38.042
Where one feels good,

00:31:38.162 --> 00:31:38.943
That is the homeland.

00:31:39.903 --> 00:31:40.403
And

00:31:40.543 --> 00:31:43.764
Liffard was fine in a studio or on stage.

00:31:45.205 --> 00:31:47.205
Drawn by the light of the lounges and reception rooms,

00:31:47.985 --> 00:31:59.109
Serge Liffard flits about and makes friends with stars of his stature like Josephine Baker or with emblematic artists of the Third Reich like the sculptor Arnaud Brecker.

00:32:08.181 --> 00:32:13.364
It was in a spirit of reaction that Serge Liffard created his most famous ballet in 1943.

00:32:14.265 --> 00:32:14.805
"Suite in white".

00:32:17.566 --> 00:32:19.948
Still performed today on the world's greatest stages,

00:32:20.468 --> 00:32:21.068
"Suite in white"

00:32:21.228 --> 00:32:22.949
is a real slap in the face to the Nazi regime.

00:32:24.050 --> 00:32:29.073
irrefutable proof of the choreographer's commitment to elegance and freedom.

00:32:57.509 --> 00:33:01.171
I remember very well what Serge Liffard said,

00:33:02.312 --> 00:33:04.153
"A suit in white is a gem."

00:33:05.754 --> 00:33:11.457
"Suit en blanc must be danced by the greatest dancers on the planet."

00:33:30.053 --> 00:33:31.494
You don't really have a story,

00:33:32.494 --> 00:33:33.595
It's a white broom.

00:33:34.756 --> 00:33:36.297
but with many variations,

00:33:37.477 --> 00:33:38.118
steps of 2,

00:33:38.518 --> 00:33:39.258
steps of 3,

00:33:39.419 --> 00:33:40.219
steps of 5,

00:33:40.759 --> 00:33:43.461
And all of this is accurate to the millimeter.

00:33:45.062 --> 00:33:46.543
And that's what makes this broom so powerful.

00:34:12.687 --> 00:34:16.729
Suite en blanc is the quintessence of the neoclassical style introduced by the Ballets Russes.

00:34:17.770 --> 00:34:18.850
This neoclassical style,

00:34:19.250 --> 00:34:20.931
inextricably linked to the name of Serge Liffard,

00:34:21.431 --> 00:34:24.073
stands out from the linear style of 19th-century ballet.

00:34:24.913 --> 00:34:28.118
It incorporates angular shapes and broken joints,

00:34:28.739 --> 00:34:29.541
very expressive.

00:34:29.821 --> 00:34:30.162
And that,

00:34:30.162 --> 00:34:35.290
That was, after all, his great choreographic idea.

00:34:35.610 --> 00:34:37.533
where he exceeded the standards,

00:34:37.553 --> 00:34:38.014
if you wish

00:34:38.014 --> 00:34:38.575
academics.

00:34:39.256 --> 00:34:47.202
The dancer's pointed position with his arm extended, his head like that, and his body slightly offset,

00:34:48.082 --> 00:34:50.244
We immediately say it's the Lipharian style.

00:35:01.772 --> 00:35:02.673
In sadness,

00:35:04.034 --> 00:35:04.835
in a fit of anger,

00:35:05.955 --> 00:35:06.556
joyfully.

00:35:07.580 --> 00:35:15.963
From that position, we can move to all the feelings we might want to convey with that position.

00:35:16.543 --> 00:35:17.763
That's the thing.

00:35:17.764 --> 00:35:19.824
And it still looks really good.

00:35:20.464 --> 00:35:21.324
The variation,

00:35:21.464 --> 00:35:22.244
cigarettes

00:35:22.385 --> 00:35:24.545
which is called in ballets,

00:35:24.605 --> 00:35:27.126
She is alive.

00:35:27.206 --> 00:35:30.167
This choreography is contemporary.

00:35:30.247 --> 00:35:32.868
Very beautiful and complicated.

00:36:09.398 --> 00:36:12.339
Suite en blanc was represented abroad during the occupation.

00:36:13.520 --> 00:36:14.500
In the autumn of 1943,

00:36:14.540 --> 00:36:15.920
November 27th and 28th,

00:36:16.460 --> 00:36:21.242
Two performances with Liffard and other dancers from the opera took place at the Deutsch Opernaus in Berlin.

00:36:21.982 --> 00:36:22.482
Peretti,

00:36:23.022 --> 00:36:24.483
Chauviré and Schwartz.

00:36:25.623 --> 00:36:29.244
It was on this occasion that Serge Liffard met the dictator for the second time.

00:36:30.164 --> 00:36:32.825
He was impressed by the piercing gaze,

00:36:32.885 --> 00:36:35.326
Hitler's piercing eyes when he stared at me.

00:36:35.746 --> 00:36:37.167
He told me it was quite impressive.

00:36:38.547 --> 00:36:44.850
This interview continues to tarnish Serge Liffard's reputation and his relationship with one of the Gestapo countesses.

00:36:45.310 --> 00:36:46.090
Sonia Olinska,

00:36:46.411 --> 00:36:47.831
bury it for good.

00:36:49.132 --> 00:36:50.772
At a reception given by the countess,

00:36:51.293 --> 00:36:53.594
He is indeed seen on her arm wearing the German uniform.

00:36:54.734 --> 00:36:56.295
Liffard was not a cautious person.

00:36:57.535 --> 00:36:59.176
I think he wasn't thinking very far ahead.

00:36:59.736 --> 00:37:00.356
But from there,

00:37:00.456 --> 00:37:01.077
He never was,

00:37:01.357 --> 00:37:02.397
In my opinion, at least.

00:37:02.398 --> 00:37:03.898
And based on the research I've done,

00:37:04.138 --> 00:37:05.959
an active collaborator.

00:37:06.699 --> 00:37:08.240
He was more of a passive collaborator who,

00:37:08.440 --> 00:37:09.441
Yes,
was celebrated.

00:37:09.881 --> 00:37:10.401
guest,

00:37:10.921 --> 00:37:11.421
There.

00:37:13.522 --> 00:37:14.183
Radio London,

00:37:14.743 --> 00:37:16.404
a major institution of the resistance,

00:37:16.864 --> 00:37:18.525
criticizes the choreographer's behavior.

00:37:19.905 --> 00:37:20.706
The blow falls preemptively.

00:37:21.926 --> 00:37:25.348
Serge Liffard was sentenced to death by the Free French and also by the Gestapo.

00:37:27.068 --> 00:37:29.650
This perfectly illustrates the character's ambivalence at that time.

00:37:30.370 --> 00:37:31.811
whom both sides believed to be the enemy.

00:37:32.832 --> 00:37:34.053
They were there as conquerors.

00:37:34.493 --> 00:37:35.494
What could they do?

00:37:36.174 --> 00:37:37.155
Where could they go?

00:37:37.715 --> 00:37:38.356
To Patride.

00:37:38.936 --> 00:37:43.620
Above all, he wanted to save his ballet company and his building.

00:37:43.820 --> 00:37:44.181
the opera.

00:37:44.181 --> 00:37:46.882
1944.

00:37:47.963 --> 00:37:51.165
The balance of power shifted in favor of Free France and its allies.

00:37:52.406 --> 00:37:55.548
Paris was liberated in August and the hunt for collaborators began.

00:37:56.496 --> 00:37:57.436
The purge begins.

00:37:58.497 --> 00:38:00.537
Unlike others who are executed without trial,

00:38:01.097 --> 00:38:03.398
Serge Liffard will benefit from a form of trial.

00:38:05.839 --> 00:38:09.520
When we look at the purge committee of that time,

00:38:10.880 --> 00:38:14.481
Today it would be a bit funny because it's really all the worst ballets.

00:38:14.821 --> 00:38:18.942
the opera's clowns who were there and who decided the lives of others.

00:38:20.142 --> 00:38:21.083
The verdict is in.

00:38:22.343 --> 00:38:25.984
Liffard was banned for life from the opera and all state theaters.

00:38:26.792 --> 00:38:27.893
by the purge committee.

00:38:34.756 --> 00:38:36.376
A collective amnesia is in place.

00:38:37.437 --> 00:38:43.159
These works are removed from the opera repertoire and the name of IFAR is removed from the posters of those that are still programmed.

00:38:52.423 --> 00:38:53.984
From May 1945 onwards,

00:38:54.708 --> 00:38:57.370
We are witnessing a battle over Liffard and his rehabilitation.

00:38:58.371 --> 00:38:58.931
The leaflets,

00:38:59.291 --> 00:39:01.753
Letters of support or condemnation are pouring in from all sides.

00:39:02.134 --> 00:39:05.896
The two opposing sides that clashed during the sham trial of Serge Liffard are facing off again.

00:39:07.357 --> 00:39:07.798
Yes,

00:39:07.798 --> 00:39:08.179
Of course,

00:39:08.179 --> 00:39:13.882
there were still lingering traces of that history of the war and then of the so-called collaboration.

00:39:14.142 --> 00:39:18.606
So there were obviously still some reservations on the technical side.

00:39:18.966 --> 00:39:21.208
We made this move.

00:39:21.844 --> 00:39:24.345
Against the stagehands who didn't want him to return to the opera,

00:39:24.845 --> 00:39:26.605
and we secured his return to the opera.

00:39:26.845 --> 00:39:30.987
It was a big fight involving all the great dancers of the time.

00:39:31.067 --> 00:39:35.608
It was a movement of passion for dance through Serge Lipin.

00:39:36.848 --> 00:39:40.229
The famous choreographer will appear before the purge committee a second time.

00:39:41.390 --> 00:39:42.010
The certificate,

00:39:42.510 --> 00:39:44.330
dated June 19, 1946,

00:39:44.911 --> 00:39:47.391
reveals that Hitler's reception at the opera was a fiction.

00:39:48.712 --> 00:39:49.632
Following its re-examination...

00:39:50.304 --> 00:39:52.225
The choreographer's sentence is reduced to just one year.

00:39:52.646 --> 00:39:53.626
with retroactive effect.

00:39:55.027 --> 00:39:56.729
November 1, 1945,

00:39:57.389 --> 00:40:00.712
Serge Liffard is therefore free to resume his activities on French territory.

00:40:01.532 --> 00:40:02.373
but on two conditions

00:40:03.133 --> 00:40:06.576
that he does not dance and that he does not take a bow on stage at the end of the ballets.

00:40:09.968 --> 00:40:11.609
All the stars and the entire top tier of the ballet,

00:40:11.889 --> 00:40:14.910
They wanted their ballet master because their ballet master,

00:40:15.370 --> 00:40:16.451
He created roles for them.

00:40:17.771 --> 00:40:18.712
He fed them.

00:40:18.872 --> 00:40:20.492
We were still in the big bowl,

00:40:20.952 --> 00:40:22.593
in the old ballets.

00:40:22.653 --> 00:40:23.754
We wanted something else.

00:40:23.874 --> 00:40:28.055
We wanted Liffard to come back and do his choreography at the opera.

00:40:28.315 --> 00:40:29.016
That was mainly it,

00:40:29.196 --> 00:40:30.496
He wasn't the dancer we wanted.

00:40:30.736 --> 00:40:31.497
He was the master.

00:40:33.057 --> 00:40:39.080
Serge Liffard thus leaves the new Monte Carlo ballet which he had joined in 1945 and returns to the opera.

00:40:39.460 --> 00:40:40.901
But only as a ballet master.

00:40:42.082 --> 00:40:44.023
The rumor spread quickly through the building.

00:40:45.103 --> 00:40:45.784
Something is there,

00:40:46.104 --> 00:40:46.604
in the air.

00:40:47.265 --> 00:40:48.185
The master is back.

00:40:49.086 --> 00:40:49.886
He is my master.

00:40:49.946 --> 00:40:51.107
He was our teacher.

00:40:51.407 --> 00:40:52.688
And the word itself,

00:40:52.868 --> 00:40:53.368
master,

00:40:53.728 --> 00:40:55.349
It is no longer applied to anyone today.

00:40:56.250 --> 00:41:02.393
When you see a person enter who has the bearing of a nobleman,

00:41:02.654 --> 00:41:06.736
because he had a distinctive approach.

00:41:07.692 --> 00:41:09.573
And to arrive with slicked-back hair,

00:41:09.893 --> 00:41:10.513
blacks,

00:41:12.794 --> 00:41:13.294
ebony,

00:41:13.335 --> 00:41:13.835
like that,

00:41:14.175 --> 00:41:14.795
in the studio,

00:41:16.396 --> 00:41:17.276
to give the impression...

00:41:18.977 --> 00:41:20.238
I don't know how to say it,

00:41:20.278 --> 00:41:23.419
That's the aura he had when he arrived.

00:41:23.659 --> 00:41:30.963
I,
witnessed the respect shown to Serge Leffard when he arrived in a rehearsal room.

00:41:32.903 --> 00:41:33.784
This generosity.

00:41:35.404 --> 00:41:42.347
He would arrive with his sheet music and he was a very, very, very musical person and everyone,

00:41:42.707 --> 00:41:56.393
We were constantly energized.

00:42:07.999 --> 00:42:10.060
In the rotunda where Liffard gives his dance classes,

00:42:10.861 --> 00:42:13.343
The choreographer emphasizes the technique that he loves so much.

00:42:14.504 --> 00:42:15.645
He likes complex figures.

00:42:15.945 --> 00:42:16.646
virtuosity.

00:42:17.606 --> 00:42:20.289
He also ensures that the performers become one with the music.

00:42:20.729 --> 00:42:21.470
dancing in rhythm,

00:42:22.390 --> 00:42:23.571
with regard to the one who deviates from it.

00:42:30.457 --> 00:42:31.017
From time to time,

00:42:31.018 --> 00:42:32.038
He would fly into rages.

00:42:33.099 --> 00:42:33.700
I could hear it...

00:42:34.520 --> 00:42:36.621
So he stamped his feet and he even said

00:42:37.722 --> 00:42:38.222
" Shit !

00:42:39.323 --> 00:42:40.243
"Shut up, damn it!"

00:42:40.263 --> 00:42:40.763
he said.

00:42:41.444 --> 00:42:46.226
He kept complaining about the dancers who weren't in sync with the music.

00:42:46.807 --> 00:42:47.307
"Oh dear!"

00:42:48.768 --> 00:42:50.409
he was saying swear words like "Shit,

00:42:50.469 --> 00:42:51.649
Grandma!

00:42:52.410 --> 00:42:53.450
"Go dance like a ghoul!"

00:42:53.470 --> 00:42:53.970
he said.

00:42:54.451 --> 00:42:55.171
"Go dance like a ghoul!"

00:42:55.752 --> 00:42:56.332
because for him,

00:42:56.892 --> 00:42:57.392
in ghoul,

00:42:57.693 --> 00:43:00.934
That was truly the end of the artist.

00:43:16.072 --> 00:43:17.133
In 1947,

00:43:17.793 --> 00:43:19.534
Serge Liffard created the ballet Les Mirages.

00:43:20.035 --> 00:43:22.416
a choreographic extravaganza in one act and two scenes.

00:43:28.040 --> 00:43:29.221
He likes to tell a story.

00:43:29.261 --> 00:43:29.801
Liffard,

00:43:29.841 --> 00:43:31.202
He is a narrative choreographer.

00:43:31.403 --> 00:43:31.983
Dance,

00:43:32.443 --> 00:43:33.964
It was used to tell a story.

00:43:34.345 --> 00:43:34.845
There.

00:43:35.045 --> 00:43:36.226
And all of Liffard's ballets,

00:43:36.266 --> 00:43:37.287
apart from Suite en Blanc,

00:43:37.387 --> 00:43:39.108
These are stories he tells.

00:43:41.910 --> 00:43:43.711
Inspired by surrealism and romance,

00:43:44.552 --> 00:43:45.052
the ballet...

00:43:45.548 --> 00:43:47.410
It tells the story of a man's relationship with his shadow.

00:43:49.071 --> 00:43:52.214
Leaving this one behind to pursue dreams of love and glory in the abode of the Moon,

00:43:53.135 --> 00:43:56.378
He finally joins her when the dreams appear in their true form.

00:43:56.838 --> 00:43:57.599
that of Mirage.

00:44:11.596 --> 00:44:12.396
It is 11:30 AM.

00:44:13.577 --> 00:44:20.939
They went up to the Opera studio at midnight and worked until 2 a.m.

00:44:21.579 --> 00:44:22.079
That,

00:44:22.379 --> 00:44:24.640
It's the dancers' discipline.

00:44:25.180 --> 00:44:27.041
It's the taste of the work of great dancers.

00:44:28.201 --> 00:44:29.782
February 2, 1949,

00:44:30.642 --> 00:44:33.263
Serge Liffard thus returns to the stage of the Opera as a dancer.

00:44:34.803 --> 00:44:36.264
His return is first and foremost a symbol.

00:44:37.144 --> 00:44:38.164
He was 44 years old at the time.

00:44:38.524 --> 00:44:41.025
a difficult age to practice such a demanding discipline,

00:44:41.325 --> 00:44:42.086
but flexibility,

00:44:42.446 --> 00:44:44.026
technique and physical strength.

00:44:44.707 --> 00:44:47.868
The last six years of his career would take the form of a fight.

00:44:49.168 --> 00:44:55.210
Serge Liffard danced until the end of his abilities.

00:44:55.790 --> 00:44:56.290
Maybe,

00:44:56.730 --> 00:44:57.251
I would say,

00:44:59.631 --> 00:45:00.131
too much.

00:45:01.728 --> 00:45:07.832
And perhaps to leave behind images of him that weren't necessarily the person he was.

00:45:08.112 --> 00:45:10.774
And we kept telling him how wonderful he was.

00:45:11.094 --> 00:45:13.576
So he still believed he was wonderful.

00:45:14.499 --> 00:45:18.580
And that's tragic because no matter how much we told him, "Master,

00:45:18.581 --> 00:45:19.620
You shouldn't do that.

00:45:20.061 --> 00:45:20.402
Master,

00:45:20.402 --> 00:45:21.841
You shouldn't dance that.

00:45:22.141 --> 00:45:23.922
He didn't understand.

00:45:27.503 --> 00:45:29.343
On December 5, 1956,

00:45:29.923 --> 00:45:31.584
Marc ceases to be a godfather on the national stage.

00:45:33.304 --> 00:45:35.465
He plays the role of Albrecht in Giselle,

00:45:35.845 --> 00:45:37.045
accompanied by Yvette Chauviré.

00:45:38.206 --> 00:45:39.626
The performance is a triumph.

00:45:40.606 --> 00:45:42.527
IFAR is called back twelve times on the opera stage.

00:45:43.575 --> 00:45:44.495
The dancer is applauded.

00:45:44.856 --> 00:45:45.456
his career,

00:45:45.936 --> 00:45:46.536
his audacity,

00:45:46.976 --> 00:45:47.476
his talent,

00:45:48.097 --> 00:45:49.457
his dedication and longevity.

00:45:51.578 --> 00:45:53.759
Some are even complaining about the somewhat late release.

00:45:56.740 --> 00:46:02.063
I experienced the greatest moments of joy of my life here.

00:46:03.083 --> 00:46:09.446
My entire creative life has passed within these walls.

00:46:10.366 --> 00:46:10.866
And then,

00:46:11.587 --> 00:46:12.307
naturally...

00:46:13.475 --> 00:46:16.776
I experienced intense bursts of joy,

00:46:17.156 --> 00:46:18.256
recognition

00:46:18.496 --> 00:46:19.477
admiration.

00:46:20.717 --> 00:46:21.477
And at the same time,

00:46:22.578 --> 00:46:26.579
I had days of conspiracy.

00:46:27.339 --> 00:46:28.079
Two years later,

00:46:28.880 --> 00:46:31.480
after some outbursts which IFAR cloaks itself in pride,

00:46:32.381 --> 00:46:35.081
He is also thanked by the opera house for his role as ballet master.

00:46:35.561 --> 00:46:37.062
Thirty years in a house,

00:46:37.742 --> 00:46:40.763
We get tired of people and eventually...

00:46:41.567 --> 00:46:41.947
I mean,

00:46:41.947 --> 00:46:42.948
But why are they still here?

00:46:43.168 --> 00:46:45.430
I mean, it's...

00:46:45.431 --> 00:46:46.130
And I think that's it.

00:46:46.230 --> 00:46:46.951
after a while,

00:46:47.371 --> 00:46:48.372
We'd had enough.

00:46:48.732 --> 00:46:49.532
the management,

00:46:51.013 --> 00:46:52.374
even the ballet masters,

00:46:52.675 --> 00:46:54.015
Perhaps the dancers too,

00:46:54.756 --> 00:46:56.757
to always have the same ballet master,

00:46:56.857 --> 00:46:58.939
to always have the same person.

00:46:59.259 --> 00:47:00.440
They wanted change.

00:47:00.560 --> 00:47:01.941
They wanted something else.

00:47:02.101 --> 00:47:03.182
It endured,

00:47:04.042 --> 00:47:05.023
He stayed for a long time.

00:47:07.239 --> 00:47:09.180
But I believe that at some point,

00:47:09.460 --> 00:47:09.960
the French,

00:47:09.960 --> 00:47:10.500
Unfortunately,

00:47:10.780 --> 00:47:12.160
are not so conservative.

00:47:12.180 --> 00:47:13.241
These are religious groups.

00:47:13.261 --> 00:47:13.801
They eat everything.

00:47:14.381 --> 00:47:15.641
And they were fed up with IFAR,

00:47:15.661 --> 00:47:16.602
They wanted something else.

00:47:17.162 --> 00:47:17.662
And then,

00:47:17.882 --> 00:47:21.663
You had young choreographers coming onto the scene.

00:47:21.683 --> 00:47:35.267
In France ?

00:47:36.531 --> 00:47:37.452
The heirs,

00:47:37.472 --> 00:47:38.032
at least,

00:47:38.473 --> 00:47:42.036
choreographers who distinguished themselves after the Diffard period,

00:47:42.156 --> 00:47:42.656
Nothing,

00:47:43.137 --> 00:47:46.179
It's obviously Roland Petit and Maurice Béjart.

00:47:46.700 --> 00:47:49.562
So Roland Petit doesn't accept it at all.

00:47:49.563 --> 00:47:53.785
He said some very unpleasant things about Serge Diffard.

00:47:54.186 --> 00:47:57.028
And yet it was Diffard who had initially brought him out.

00:47:57.428 --> 00:47:58.029
as a dancer,

00:47:58.389 --> 00:47:59.110
and on the other hand

00:47:59.670 --> 00:48:01.532
Maurice Béjart always claimed it.

00:48:09.230 --> 00:48:13.853
He inspired these two choreographers to pursue choreography.

00:48:15.314 --> 00:48:23.220
I remember Roland saying that Liffard's choreography was outdated.

00:48:23.221 --> 00:48:25.761
that it was no longer possible to see Liffard's choreographies,

00:48:26.882 --> 00:48:29.184
but he drew a lot of inspiration from it.

00:48:47.007 --> 00:48:49.988
Which Béjart revolutionized with his Rite of Spring,

00:48:50.348 --> 00:48:53.049
by putting the dancers in tight-fitting academic costumes and all that,

00:48:53.469 --> 00:48:55.150
It had already been done with Serge Liffard.

00:49:18.343 --> 00:49:18.924
When

00:49:19.624 --> 00:49:21.246
Liffard has left the Paris Opera.

00:49:21.406 --> 00:49:21.886
forced,

00:49:21.886 --> 00:49:24.308
because his contract was coming to an end,

00:49:24.809 --> 00:49:29.973
For him, it was a truly extremely difficult period.

00:49:30.233 --> 00:49:31.034
He said,

00:49:31.174 --> 00:49:33.156
He finds it in the newspaper.

00:49:33.576 --> 00:49:34.857
"I am guillotined."

00:49:35.197 --> 00:49:36.458
Yes,
they cut off his head.

00:49:36.739 --> 00:49:38.020
He could no longer be on stage,

00:49:38.040 --> 00:49:39.241
He could no longer be behind the scenes.

00:49:39.242 --> 00:49:40.922
He could no longer be in the Opera studios,

00:49:41.383 --> 00:49:42.904
He was practically dead.

00:49:43.244 --> 00:49:45.586
He had experienced fabulous triumphs and successes,

00:49:45.587 --> 00:49:46.187
It must also be said.

00:49:46.807 --> 00:49:47.628
in this theatre,

00:49:47.768 --> 00:49:50.270
So he was very attached.

00:49:50.990 --> 00:49:52.311
But it's true that he spent his life there.

00:49:52.312 --> 00:49:53.452
He arrived at 8 a.m.

00:49:53.492 --> 00:49:55.194
He left again at 8am/midnight.

00:49:56.054 --> 00:49:58.496
He was losing his beloved home.

00:49:59.677 --> 00:50:00.758
And therefore also,

00:50:01.418 --> 00:50:03.440
He was losing the ability to practice his art.

00:50:03.980 --> 00:50:07.383
So he took refuge in filmmaking,

00:50:08.424 --> 00:50:11.486
He took refuge mainly in writing books.

00:50:12.187 --> 00:50:14.749
and then he saw the young dancers.

00:50:15.605 --> 00:50:16.926
And he helped them if he could.

00:50:18.227 --> 00:50:20.328
While Liffard no longer holds as much interest for the dance world,

00:50:20.949 --> 00:50:23.471
The newspapers continue to report on the actions and words of the character.

00:50:24.491 --> 00:50:29.395
It was in 1958 that Serge Liffard's last and greatest stroke of media genius took place.

00:50:30.896 --> 00:50:31.537
Serge Liffard,

00:50:32.297 --> 00:50:36.360
determined to erase in blood a slap received in public at the Champs-Élysées theatre.

00:50:37.761 --> 00:50:39.082
Drawn by the clash of weapons,

00:50:39.083 --> 00:50:43.105
a crowd of journalists held the position of the ancient heart in this tragedy in the form of a pas de deux.

00:50:44.889 --> 00:50:45.890
The origin of this duel,

00:50:46.850 --> 00:50:49.912
a plagiarism of the ballet Suite en Blanc by the Marquis de Cuevas,

00:50:50.532 --> 00:50:53.374
which produces it at the Théâtre des Champs-Elysées under the barely modified title

00:50:53.754 --> 00:50:54.254
"Black and White".

00:50:55.315 --> 00:50:56.876
The choreographer then confronts the marquis,

00:50:57.236 --> 00:50:58.116
who slaps him.

00:50:58.957 --> 00:50:59.557
Serge Liffard,

00:50:59.697 --> 00:51:00.218
due to a lack of gloves,

00:51:00.738 --> 00:51:02.679
He throws down his towel and demands retribution through a duel.

00:51:03.780 --> 00:51:06.501
Cuevas was then 73 years old and Liffard 54.

00:51:07.221 --> 00:51:08.582
We were coming down from rehearsal,

00:51:08.882 --> 00:51:11.624
There was immediately a photographer or a journalist who was there.

00:51:11.984 --> 00:51:12.544
“Mr. Liffard,

00:51:12.584 --> 00:51:12.905
master,

00:51:12.905 --> 00:51:13.425
Please ! "

00:51:13.769 --> 00:51:15.190
Would you like to give us some information?

00:51:15.250 --> 00:51:15.930
How does it work?

00:51:16.470 --> 00:51:17.231
Then he looked at me,

00:51:17.232 --> 00:51:17.732
he said,

00:51:18.331 --> 00:51:19.212
Gilbert Maillère,

00:51:19.692 --> 00:51:21.013
Go get the accessories.

00:51:21.493 --> 00:51:22.734
So I went to the accessories section,

00:51:23.054 --> 00:51:24.875
I would return with a sword or a foil.

00:51:25.215 --> 00:51:26.456
So he struck a pose like this,

00:51:26.916 --> 00:51:27.416
he said,

00:51:27.576 --> 00:51:28.677
I'm going to pierce through.

00:51:28.737 --> 00:51:29.778
Marquis, like a butterfly

00:51:30.118 --> 00:51:30.718
telegraph,

00:51:31.038 --> 00:51:31.639
marquise,

00:51:32.059 --> 00:51:32.559
New York,

00:51:32.819 --> 00:51:33.840
come and collect the corpse.

00:51:34.540 --> 00:51:35.501
The moment was near.

00:51:36.321 --> 00:51:39.503
Serge Liffard had equipped himself with a number of fetishes.

00:51:40.043 --> 00:51:40.683
Leather gloves,

00:51:40.743 --> 00:51:41.704
slippers and icons,

00:51:41.924 --> 00:51:43.285
because a bad move can happen quickly.

00:51:44.545 --> 00:51:51.049
The marquis said, "Now I'm going to skewer him," and he made this stab wound several centimeters long on his left arm.

00:51:51.529 --> 00:51:57.833
Then he told me, Liffard, that the Marquis had a fit of tears and suddenly said, "I killed my son."

00:51:58.253 --> 00:51:59.414
I killed the god of dance.

00:51:59.934 --> 00:52:02.235
Serge Liffard received the care his condition required.

00:52:02.716 --> 00:52:05.557
The Marquis de Cuevas sighed, thinking he had pierced his own son.

00:52:06.238 --> 00:52:08.619
Thank goodness everything ends with entrechats.

00:52:10.700 --> 00:52:11.761
Deprived of his passion,

00:52:12.277 --> 00:52:14.099
Serge Liffard's mood will gradually deteriorate.

00:52:15.720 --> 00:52:18.122
Even though he continues to work to promote the art of dance,

00:52:18.662 --> 00:52:19.563
The enthusiasm is no longer the same.

00:52:20.984 --> 00:52:26.989
30 years of glory have not been enough to quench his thirst for recognition and his need to express himself.

00:52:28.711 --> 00:52:31.333
She is a woman who will provide Liffard with all the support he needs.

00:52:31.853 --> 00:52:32.654
Liliane Hallefeld.

00:52:33.434 --> 00:52:39.099
I believe that Liliane Hallefeld was very quickly seduced by this dark character,

00:52:40.660 --> 00:52:41.160
Very beautiful.

00:52:41.401 --> 00:52:44.063
still very famous

00:52:44.724 --> 00:52:45.664
exciting,

00:52:45.685 --> 00:52:46.305
keen.

00:52:46.825 --> 00:52:50.288
She was his angel,

00:52:50.308 --> 00:52:51.009
his guardian.

00:52:51.369 --> 00:52:53.711
Lefarge was an emotional man.

00:52:53.771 --> 00:52:54.752
under all social stressors,

00:52:54.792 --> 00:52:58.175
like any creative person.

00:52:58.275 --> 00:53:03.219
But she was probably his soul.

00:53:03.279 --> 00:53:10.245
which gave him the power to find rest.

00:53:10.865 --> 00:53:16.509
And Liliane first took him to Cannes to separate him from his Parisian environment.

00:53:16.829 --> 00:53:17.490
and then afterwards,

00:53:17.810 --> 00:53:18.611
many years later,

00:53:18.991 --> 00:53:19.491
in Switzerland,

00:53:19.571 --> 00:53:21.533
also partly to isolate him from the Parisian scene.

00:53:21.573 --> 00:53:23.234
because Lilane wanted to have it for herself,

00:53:23.334 --> 00:53:23.834
anyway.

00:53:36.424 --> 00:53:37.945
And then he calls me one day out of the blue.

00:53:38.337 --> 00:53:42.260
I am on the terrace of the Countess's villa.

00:53:42.400 --> 00:53:44.301
Oh really?
And what do you do?

00:53:44.941 --> 00:53:48.644
I'm sweeping the terrace because it needs sweeping.

00:53:49.524 --> 00:53:52.446
And he told me, with great melancholy,

00:53:53.407 --> 00:53:53.967
You see,

00:53:54.487 --> 00:53:56.148
I am still the broomstick master.

00:53:58.685 --> 00:54:04.367
Dancer and choreographer Serge Liffard died last night in Lausanne at the age of 81.

00:54:04.688 --> 00:54:09.630
Minister François Léotard is paying tribute this evening to the last great choreographer to emerge from Diaghilev's Russian ballets.

00:54:09.970 --> 00:54:10.330
And Mr.

00:54:10.330 --> 00:54:14.312
Léotard wants the opera to maintain Liffard's work and present his best works.

00:54:15.112 --> 00:54:20.254
We visited a tomb together in Egypt.

00:54:21.275 --> 00:54:22.275
And we were together,

00:54:22.815 --> 00:54:23.315
like that,

00:54:24.116 --> 00:54:26.637
in front of the excavation of the dead body.

00:54:27.157 --> 00:54:27.657
And then,

00:54:28.179 --> 00:54:29.360
We were both sitting there,

00:54:29.801 --> 00:54:30.482
and I said to him,

00:54:31.403 --> 00:54:31.903
master,

00:54:32.185 --> 00:54:32.846
your seat,

00:54:33.026 --> 00:54:33.526
She is here.

00:54:34.368 --> 00:54:34.728
He makes me,

00:54:34.728 --> 00:54:35.630
Do you believe in life?

00:54:36.010 --> 00:54:36.510
Do you believe that?

00:54:37.372 --> 00:54:37.834
All at once,

00:54:37.834 --> 00:54:38.654
He became a pharaoh.

00:54:38.975 --> 00:54:39.475
Do you understand?

00:54:41.038 --> 00:54:41.799
He had this side to him...

